  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nEarly afternoon water vapor and RAP imagery show a closed upper low \nover southwestern Hudson Bay (500 mb heights at 10pctl of climo) \nwith strong zonal flow over the northern tier of the west half of \nthe CONUS. Band of clouds associated with departing moist axis is \nover the eastern half of the UP with clearing skies to the west. \nSurface front is coincident with this band of clouds, along which a \nband of showers/sprinkles is occurring from eastern Alger SW through \nDickinson County. Cannot rule out an isolated TS over \nSchoolcraft/Luce through early this afternoon with dewpoints in\nthe low to mid-60s and SBCAPE 500-1000 J/kg per SPC \nMesoanalysis. Otherwise, winds shift NW behind the front, \nushering in a cooler and drier airmass as lower heights \nassociated with the Hudson Bay low pivot across the upper Great \nLakes. LREF mean 850 mb temps fall to 2- 3C over Lake Superior \non Sunday, which is 15-20pctl of climo, and should result in \nsome lake-induced instability with surface to 850 mb delta-Ts \naround 13C. CAMs show lake-effect rain showers developing during\nthe late morning into afternoon, particularly over the east \nhalf as the dry column will be more prominent over the west. \n\nSunday night, ideal radiational cooling conditions develop as PWATs \nfall to 0.3-0.4 in (5-10pctl of climo) associated with a building \n~1025mb high. Have reduced lows relative to NBM guidance Monday \nmorning, with widespread 30s in the interior and patchy frost \nexpected.\n\nAnother closed upper low shifts from the northern Rockies into \ncentral Canada early next week, with a strong LLJ feeding Gulf \nmoisture poleward ahead of it. This will boost PWATs up to 1.5-1.75 \ninches Monday night, and an area of rain will move from west to east \nalong the nose of the LLJ across the UP late Monday into Monday \nnight. This looks to be a fairly quick-hitting system with most of \nthe rain having exited east of the UP by Tuesday morning, but should \nprovide at least a wetting rainfall for the entire area. A blend of \nLREF and NBM probs of > 0.50\" in 24 hours are 50-80%, and > 1.00\" \nare 30-50%, lowest over the Keweenaw and highest over the south-\ncentral. Cluster analysis shows variability with respect to precip \namounts is tied to the 500 mb heights in central Canada - a deeper \ntrough resulting in a stronger low actually results in a drier \nsolution for the UP, possibly due to the LLJ forcing moving through \nquicker.\n\nTemps should be fairly seasonable on Monday, and a sneaky warm day \nis possible Tuesday with mixing into warm temps aloft in the wake of \nthe Monday night system. The rest of the week looks fairly \nseasonable as we are favored to be on the northern extent of the \npersistent positive height anomaly over the south-central US, \nalthough proximity of a moisture plume along the edge of this \nhigh means we can't rule out some rain toward the middle/end of \nthe week.",
